Title: check-staging: an embedded PDF title is a hint, never grounds for absent
Created: 2026-11-10
Author: reviewer

--- log ---
2026-11-10T08:48Z reviewer created

--- body ---
## Context

Author ruling, 2026-11-10, verbatim: "The embedded PDF titles are
unreliable". It answers a finding gaze raised twice, non-blocking, in the
reviews of tickets 1502 and 1503: `check-staging` can still call a file
`absent` from a title search alone, when the PDF has a real-looking embedded
title and its identifiers miss.

Today (`audit_one`, ~line 2572 on 2026-11-10; `_staging_verdict`, ~2528) a
title built from the **filename** that finds nothing is `unidentified`,
never `absent` (ticket 1456). A title read from the PDF **container**
(`_pdf_info`, ~2511) is trusted more: a miss on it can conclude `absent`,
and the report's action for `absent` is to create an item. Container titles
are whatever the producing tool wrote: a publisher's template string, the
title of another document, a truncated or mis-encoded line. Ticket 0081 of
sen-causal-reading was exactly this: a publisher string in /Title killed the
search for 15 files.

## Decision

A title taken from the file itself, whether from the container or from the
filename, is a search hint. A search on it may return candidates; a miss on
it alone is `unidentified`, never `absent`. Only a title someone **gave**
(a `.bib` entry, a receipt's given metadata) can, with no hit, conclude
`absent`. This is the same line 1502 and 1503 drew for identifiers: given
versus scraped.

No filtering of "junk" titles by pattern: the rule is about where the title
came from, not what it looks like.

## Exit criteria

- [ ] A PDF with a plausible embedded title that matches nothing, and no given metadata, is `unidentified`; a test shows it, with a control where a `.bib` title that matches nothing is still `absent`
- [ ] The existing filename-title behaviour is unchanged
- [ ] `skills/zotero/references/` text on verdicts agrees with the code
- [ ] `make check` passes
